<speak>
<s>From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">22032026</say-as>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>American and Israeli airstrikes have killed fourteen hundred Iranians in twenty-two days, hitting hospitals and homes across more than two hundred cities — a pace faster than civilian deaths in the opening weeks of the 2003 Iraq invasion.</s> <s>President Trump has now given Tehran forty-eight hours to reopen the Strait of Hormuz or face strikes on Iran's largest power plant — an ultimatum the Geneva Conventions would classify as targeting civilian infrastructure.</s> <s>Iran has responded by demonstrating that its missiles can reach European capitals, widening a conflict that no party seems able to contain.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>As Ukraine and Russia exchanged strikes on Sunday, ceasefire talks resumed — with Kyiv accusing Moscow of stalling while Iran-war oil revenues steadily replenish the Russian military budget.</s> <s>President <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zɛˈlɛnski">Zelenskyy</phoneme> pressed Washington to allow European allies to buy American weapons for Ukraine before this round concludes.</s> <s>Whether any agreement emerges may depend less on what is said at the table than on whether Washington applies real pressure on Moscow.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>In Lebanon, Hezbollah shelled Israeli troop positions and fired rockets across the border on Saturday — eighteen days into a conflict that has killed more than a thousand people, among them a hundred and eighteen children and forty healthcare workers.</s> <s>Despite a sustained Israeli air campaign, Hezbollah remains operational while Israel fights simultaneously on three fronts: Gaza, Lebanon, and Iran.</s> <s>Israel's military chief warned that operations in Lebanon will escalate in the coming week.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Fifty-two people were killed in a single night in Zike village, in Nigeria's Plateau State — the latest toll in a conflict that now kills more Nigerians annually than Boko Haram did at its peak.</s> <s>Saharan desertification is pushing Fulani pastoralists into farming communities' ancestral land across three northern states as usable pasture disappears.</s> <s>Two hundred American troops are in Nigeria training the military to fight Islamist groups — not to protect the communities dying in this climate-driven land war.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Pakistan has overtaken Syria and Somalia to lead the Global Terrorism Index, with terror deaths rising six percent in a single year.</s> <s>The <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="tɛˈriːk ɪ ˈtælɪbɑːn">Tehrik-i-Taliban</phoneme> Pakistan gained a cross-border sanctuary when the Taliban returned to power in Afghanistan in 2021, reversing nearly a decade of declining attacks inside Pakistan.</s> <s>Pakistan's army is escalating military operations in <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈkaɪbər pækˈtʊŋkwɑː">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</phoneme> while the militant group continues to recruit across the border.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Petrol costs twenty-five percent more in Colombo since Sunday, after Sri Lanka raised fuel prices in response to supply disruptions on routes passing through the Strait of Hormuz.</s> <s>The country imports nearly all its fuel, and the International Monetary Fund recovery plan that pulled Sri Lanka back from default in 2022 contains no buffer for sustained energy shocks.</s> <s>Unions that brought down former President <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="rɑːdʒəˈpæksə">Rajapaksa</phoneme> are now demanding the fuel subsidies that his successor, President <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="dɪˈsænəjɑːkə">Dissanayake</phoneme>, cannot afford.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Ninety percent of Japan's crude oil passes through the Strait of Hormuz — and Tokyo is now weighing whether to send minesweepers into waters it cannot secure on its own.</s> <s>The government is framing the potential mission as non-combat, the only framing that avoids a direct conflict with Japan's pacifist constitution, which has restricted offensive military operations since 1947.</s> <s>No commitment has been made, and any deployment depends on a ceasefire that Japan cannot bring about.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Chinese Premier Li Qiang told international executives this week that protectionism, in his words, solves nothing.</s> <s>Hours later, Beijing published a five-year plan elevating commodity stockpiling to national security — prioritising semiconductor equipment, industrial software, and aerospace engines: the exact sectors where United States export controls have cut Chinese access.</s> <s>The message was unmistakable — China preaches open markets while systematically building the capacity to survive without them.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Brazil pledged to cut greenhouse gas emissions between forty-nine and fifty-eight percent by 2035, one of the most ambitious national targets submitted to the United Nations this year.</s> <s>The same week, Brazil's soy industry scrapped its voluntary deforestation moratorium, removing the private-sector compliance that gave the government's targets their credibility.</s> <s>Brazil's environmental enforcement agency must now sanction agribusiness violations with fewer staff and a smaller budget than it had five years ago.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>New Zealand swapped binding emissions reductions for a pine plantation programme — and climate lawyers have taken the government to court over it.</s> <s>Courts must decide whether planting fire-prone trees legally satisfies New Zealand's obligations under its own climate legislation, a question with no settled precedent anywhere in the world.</s> <s>Litigators in Australia and the United Kingdom are watching closely: a ruling against New Zealand could become a template for challenging similar substitutions elsewhere.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>South Korea ranked fourth in global artificial intelligence adoption, while the United States placed twentieth, according to a new report by venture firm <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈændriːsən ˈhɔːrəwɪts">Andreessen Horowitz</phoneme>.</s> <s>Three South Korean applications entered the global top fifty most-used artificial intelligence products; Singapore leads the world overall.</s> <s>The rankings disrupt a widely held assumption — that leadership in artificial intelligence and leadership in Silicon Valley are the same thing.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>A San Francisco jury found Elon Musk liable for deceiving Twitter shareholders during the forty-four billion dollar acquisition.</s> <s>Two posts claiming the deal was first on hold, then could not proceed, wiped two point six billion dollars from shareholder value in a single day — statements the jury found were deliberately false.</s> <s>A separate court proceeding will set the damages Musk must pay, while he simultaneously holds senior advisory positions in the United States federal government.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.</s>
</speak>